<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guna Yala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as San Blas, Kuna Yala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guna Yala, formerly San Blas, is an indigenous territory (comarca) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guna-people">Guna people</a> along Panama's north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast, including some 365 islands. After the Guna Revolution of 1925 against attempts to suppress their customs, the Guna gained autonomy, recognised in law in 1938 and 1953. It is governed by the Guna General Congress and is known for its molas, colourful textile panels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guna-yala.jpg" alt="Guna Yala" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olosipu at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kuna_Yala_beach_2003.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
